---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { substack, substackPath } from "../data/elsewhere";
const essay = `${substack}/p/the-real-problem-with-ai-coding-isnt`;
const demo = "/foundry/demo/";
const art = "/foundry/art";
---
<Base
  signoff
  title="Foundry: the voice of your project · inixiative"
  description="Foundry is the workspace around your agent. What you've already decided reaches every session, on the Claude Code and Codex subscriptions you already pay for."
  domain="agentic"
  current="agentic"
>
  <section class="hero hero-art">
    <div class="hero-copy">
    <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot" data-live></span>Foundry</span>
    <h1 class="display-1">Stop repeating yourself to Claude.</h1>
    <p class="subhead">The voice of your project.</p>
    <p class="lede">
      Coding agents keep writing their own versions of what your codebase already has. Foundry is the workspace around your agent: Claude Code and Codex, and apps that speak
      ACP. What you've already decided reaches every session.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="https://github.com/inixiative/foundry">Foundry on GitHub</a>
      <a class="button button-secondary" href={demo}>Try the demo</a>
      <a class="text-link substack-link" href={essay}>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d={substackPath} /></svg>
        The real problem with AI coding is alignment
      </a>
    </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="art art-wide">
      <img
        src={`${art}/foundry-1200.webp`}
        srcset={`${art}/foundry-800.webp 800w, ${art}/foundry-1200.webp 1200w`}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 50vw"
        width="1200"
        height="675"
        alt="Illustration of a neon-lit workshop at night: a glowing blue wireframe structure rises over a ring of workbenches, screens and machines."
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="text">
        <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>The problem</span>
        <h2 class="display-3">Every session starts half-amnesiac.</h2>
        <p>
          You pay a re-alignment tax: you explain your conventions, your past decisions and your taste
          again. The model is outside your control, and so is the harness it runs in.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your corpus is the one layer you control: your docs, your rules, your <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.
          Foundry is a tool for controlling it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="text">
        <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>The bet</span>
        <h2 class="display-3">Told the right thing, the agent does what you want.</h2>
        <p>
          The bet is that your corrections compile into docs that tell it automatically. In early runs, when
          Aron's corrections reached the agent before it planned, the plans came out right. Oracle runs the
          loop that tunes those docs.
        </p>
        <p>
          The longer version is the essay{" "}<a class="substack-link" href={essay}><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="13" height="13" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d={substackPath} /></svg>The Real Problem With AI Coding Isn't Capability. It's Alignment.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>What it does</span>
      <h2>Five things Foundry is for.</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="pitches">
      <li class="pitch">
        <h3>Stop repeating yourself to Claude.</h3>
        <p>
          What you've already decided reaches every session. Foundry splits what your project knows across
          small agents called Wardens, each holding one part. When your request touches a Warden's part, it
          reminds your agent what already exists and where it's written down. Wardens never block; your
          agent decides what to use.
        </p>
        <p>Wardens are built from your repo's docs, one per topic, and run once the project turns them on in <code>.foundry/project.json</code>. A fresh install starts without them. Repos built from template ship with their Wardens built and turned on.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${demo}?pitch=repeat`}>Watch it in the demo</a>
      </li>
      <li class="pitch">
        <h3>Every agent you pay for, in one place.</h3>
        <p>
          Claude Code and Codex on your own subscriptions. Each thread picks its worker, and every
          session is captured.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="pitch">
        <h3>Stop being a meat proxy.</h3>
        <p>Your agent, their agent and the people in one thread, hashing out the work.</p>
        <div class="tags"><span class="tag" data-tone="open">Host side built</span><span class="tag" data-tone="progress">Joiner view in progress</span></div>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${demo}?pitch=proxy`}>Watch it in the demo</a>
      </li>
      <li class="pitch pitch-art">
        <img
          class="pitch-portrait"
          src={`${art}/herald-360.webp`}
          width="360"
          height="480"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Illustration of a Herald: a silver-haired runner with winged boots leaps across a neon city skyline, holding a staff toward a glowing antenna dish."
        />
        <h3>Stop checking a dozen apps.</h3>
        <p>Whatever comes in (a Slack DM, a review request, an email) reaches you already researched and in context. It's read as data, so it can't instruct your agent.</p>
        <div class="tags"><span class="tag" data-tone="progress">In progress</span></div>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${demo}?pitch=apps`}>Watch it in the demo</a>
      </li>
      <li class="pitch">
        <h3>Set up your repo.</h3>
        <p>Foundry reads your repo and builds the docs and Wardens.</p>
        <div class="tags"><span class="tag" data-tone="progress">In progress</span></div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="warden-intro">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>How Wardens work</span>
        <h2>Each Warden holds one part of what your project knows.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Foundry splits your docs and code by topic and gives each topic to one Warden. A Warden speaks up only
          when your request touches its topic, and makes sure your agent is reminded of it. Building Wardens
          for any repo is pitch 5, in progress.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="art art-portrait">
        <img
          src={`${art}/warden-600.webp`}
          srcset={`${art}/warden-360.webp 360w, ${art}/warden-600.webp 600w`}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 60vw, 18rem"
          width="600"
          height="800"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Illustration of a Warden: an armoured guard in a long black coat stands at a red, hexagon-patterned firewall gate, holding a long rifle."
        />
      </figure>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>You ask in plain words.</strong> In Claude Code, Codex or an ACP app, the way you already work.</li>
      <li><strong>Wardens notice what it touches.</strong> Each one checks whether the request is in its part of your codebase.</li>
      <li><strong>They remind your agent of what exists.</strong> The primitive, the pattern, and the doc section where it's written down. They never block.</li>
      <li><strong>Your agent decides.</strong> It builds with that in view, on your machine.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="text">
        <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Runs on your subscription</span>
        <h2 class="display-3">It runs on your subscriptions, with no API key.</h2>
        <p>
          Foundry runs on your machine and uses the Claude Code and Codex logins you already have.
          Every session runs in the real harness, on your own subscription. Kingdom can hold those logins
          for you and give each project its own pool.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="text">
        <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>License</span>
        <h2 class="display-3">Source-available, and MIT in April 2030.</h2>
        <p>
          Foundry is under BSL 1.1. It is free to use, including at work. You may not resell it. It
          becomes MIT in April 2030. The core engine and the ACP adapter are MIT today.
        </p>
        <p>Thanks to <a href="https://userevidence.com">UserEvidence</a> for supporting open-source software.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Run it</span>
      <h2>Clone it and set it up.</h2>
    </div>
    <pre class="code"><span class="prompt">$ </span>git clone https://github.com/inixiative/foundry.git
<span class="prompt">$ </span>cd foundry &amp;&amp; bun install
<span class="prompt">$ </span>bun run setup
<span class="prompt">$ </span>bun run start</pre>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="https://github.com/inixiative/foundry">Foundry on GitHub</a>
      <a class="text-link" href="/agentic/">The rest of the agentic tools</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
